def P(num,h,fig,tier,grant,inv,title,teaches,figs,status="Expired",**k):
    d=dict(num=num,hash=h,fig=fig,tier=tier,grant=grant,inv=inv,title=title,teaches=teaches,figs=figs,status=status); d.update(k); return d
INTRO = "<p>Before sewers reached most homes, inventors patented dry-earth closets, privies, washboards, hand washing machines and wringers. Early septic tanks soon followed. These drawings run from a bucket-and-dry-earth toilet anyone can build, through concrete privy tanks a small crew can cast, to the engineered septic tanks of the first sewage works. Use them with the latrine, waste and laundry chapters of this guide. Keep every toilet, pit or tank well away from, and downhill of, wells and springs.</p>"
UNPATENTED = ["The pit latrine, the dug cat-hole, the wash tub and plunger, and home-made lye soap are old common practice and free to copy."]
PATENTS = [
P("US91474","55/99/da/54ce7ea244dab8/US91474.pdf","b1/4a/f6/8e83f7f4600eca/US91474-drawings-page-1.png","A","1869-06-15","Henry Moule &amp; Henry John Girdlestone","Improvement in earth-closets (deodorizing apparatus)",
  "The Rev. Henry Moule's dry-earth closet. Instead of water, a pivoted hopper of dry, sifted earth behind the seat drops a charge onto each deposit when the user pulls the handle, which kills the smell and starts composting. A bucket, a box of dry soil and a scoop do the same job.","Section through the seat, earth hopper and pail"),
P("US440611","2d/4e/a4/c85f66b9ed30bd/US440611.pdf","79/48/94/a962e4e1bfe908/US440611-drawings-page-1.png","A","1890-11-11","Charles H. Leonard","Portable earth-closet",
  "A portable dry-earth closet for household use: a box commode with a removable pail and a store of dry earth to cover each use. It is a carpentry project from boards and a bucket.","Fig. 1 vertical section of the box, seat and pail"),
P("US49635","8d/96/b0/efa9d68427fc51/US49635.pdf","73/da/fa/9bca3de317f2f1/US49635-drawings-page-1.png","A","1865-08-29","John S. Lash","Wash-board",
  "A washboard with a separate, removable ribbed rubbing board set in a wooden frame. It shows the ribbed rubbing surface that does the work of hand laundry in a tub.","Fig. 1 perspective; Fig. 2 removable rubbing board; Fig. 3 section"),
P("US22236","62/5f/65/ac5a76e9b230ff/US22236.pdf","1e/2c/9f/35ff098844df50/US22236-drawings-page-1.png","A","1858-12-07","John G. Haley, Isaac Wilson &amp; Jackson Lyon","Washing-machine",
  "An Illinois hand washing machine. A ribbed rubber is swung back and forth over a ribbed horizontal washboard in a wooden box, with the ribs meshing, and a lever press at the side squeezes water out of the clothes. All of it is woodwork.","Fig. 1 perspective; Fig. 2 long section through box, board and rubber"),
P("US32475","ee/14/e2/8c70543bc619b8/US32475.pdf","a9/86/83/8dde28649eae8a/US32475-drawings-page-1.png","B","1861-06-04","George B. Griffin","Clothes-wringer",
  "A tub-mounted clothes wringer: a pair of pressure rollers turned by a crank, clamped to the rim of a wash tub. Wrung clothes dry far faster in damp or cold weather.","Fig. 1 front elevation; Fig. 2 vertical section; Fig. 3 detail"),
P("US1290886","ef/24/95/3f71e39e054a40/US1290886.pdf","d8/69/15/ca1fefa8b6b869/US1290886-drawings-page-1.png","B","1919-01-14","Warren H. Booker","Privy",
  "A sanitary privy from Raleigh, North Carolina. A cement tank set on a slab in a pit is split by a partition into two settling chambers. A concrete cover slab is both the tank lid and the privy floor, with the commode over one hole, the privy house anchored to it, and an overflow and vent pipe leading out. A small crew can cast it.","Fig. 3 tank and commode; Fig. 4 corner anchor irons; Fig. 5 overflow and vent pipe"),
P("US1281528","cc/e1/9b/47839457a0ebec/US1281528.pdf","db/d6/35/c3aa1a5f6ccaba/US1281528-drawings-page-1.png","B","1918-10-15","James B. Davis (assignor to Standard Cement Construction Co.)","Combined septic tank and privy",
  "A privy built over a small septic tank for unsewered homes, sized so it could also serve several households. The waste settles and digests in the tank before the liquid leaves.","Sections through privy, tank chambers and outlet"),
P("US634423","05/50/ef/e6c21ed706d6e2/US634423.pdf","6e/e9/4e/686b07460c0552/US634423-drawings-page-1.png","C","1899-10-03","Donald Cameron, Frederick J. Commin &amp; Arthur J. Martin (Exeter, England)","Process of and apparatus for treating sewage (septic tank)",
  "The Exeter 'septic tank' patent. Sewage flows slowly through a closed tank where bacteria liquefy the solids, and the liquid leaves by a slotted outlet below the water line, so scum and sludge stay behind. This is the idea every home septic tank uses. It was patented in the US and many other countries, including Canada.","Sections and plan of the tank, inlet and slotted outlet"),
P("US917663","5e/c1/44/85784cd3a34587/US917663.pdf","34/60/36/7d0066320b5724/US917663-drawings-page-1.png","C","1909-04-06","Henry C. Rogers (Estes Park, Colorado)","Septic tank",
  "A septic tank with internal partitions and pipes that separate the light and heavy parts of the sewage, and a vented inlet that keeps tank gases out of the house drain. It is a reference for designing baffles and venting in a cast tank.","Section through partitions, pipes and vent"),
P("US627109","a7/3f/39/f0960094eea6b2/US627109.pdf","bd/28/8b/244b16cc2a863a/US627109-drawings-page-1.png","A","1899-06-20","Alfred B. Forrest","Fly-trap",
  "A cheap baited screen trap for flies. A half-round tin base carries a flat side of wire screen and a cone-shaped screen side. The flat screen stops just short of the base, leaving a gap. A shallow bait dish slides in under that gap, so flies crawl in to reach the bait. The patent says it is meant to be simple enough for anyone used to household appliances to make and use (see 3.3 Flies, mosquitoes and rodents).","Fig. 1-2 the trap from both sides; Fig. 3 section; Fig. 4 bait dish"),
P("US349348","31/33/3a/1fb62357c8dd02/US349348.pdf","2b/4e/a6/846646ff47af3f/US349348-drawings-page-1.png","C","1886-09-21","Patrick Harvey","Water-closet (siphon closet)",
  "A siphon water-closet. The bowl and the first leg of the discharge pipe form the water-seal trap, and the discharge pipe is shaped as a siphon. Flushing water is also sent down past the outlet leg in a ring-shaped stream, which sucks air out of the siphon so that it starts and empties the bowl. This is the factory-made, piped toilet; to keep a house toilet working without power, see 2.5 Using a house toilet and septic system without power.","Fig. 1 section through bowl, trap, siphon, flushing pipe and tank; Fig. 2 adjusting detail"),
]
